ArrayList is a resizable (dynamic) array implementation of the List interface in Java
It is used when you want:

✔ Ordered data
✔ Duplicates allowed
✔ Fast access using index
✔ Dynamic size

NO synchronization (not thread-safe)

Initially the capacity of an ArrayList is 0. when you add the first element, the capacity is increased to 10.
When you add more elements than the current capacity,
the ArrayList automatically resizes itself by creating a new array with a larger capacity and copying the existing elements to it.
    newCapacity = oldCapacity + (oldCapacity / 2)

ArrayList is not synchronized, which means it is not thread-safe.
If multiple threads access an ArrayList concurrently and at least one of the threads modifies the list structurally,
it must be synchronized externally.

Single thread  → ArrayList
Multi thread   → CopyOnWriteArrayList / synchronizedList
   `public static  List<T> synchronizedList(List<T> list`
   `CopyOnWriteArrayList<T> threadSafeList = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<T>();`

copyOnWriteArrayList is a thread-safe variant of ArrayList
 but it takes more memory and is slower for write operations due to copying the entire array on each modification.

 synchronizedList is a wrapper around an ArrayList that provides synchronized access to the list.
 It is faster than CopyOnWriteArrayList for write operations but can lead to contention and reduced performance in multi-threaded environments.